Fillets and chamfers: the first end of a contact on a closed edge of a
periodic face snapped onto the edge's vertex at this end (mitcad#1117).

The case: a blind hole's rim bevelled or rounded as deep as the hole (a
plate with a hole of radius 5, 2 deep, a chamfer or fillet of 2 on its
rim). The blend takes the hole's wall away: its contact on the wall runs
along the wall's bottom edge, a whole circle closed at the seam's vertex.
ChFi3d_KParticular accepts the plane and the cylinder and
ChFiKPart_ComputeData computes the blend; SplitKPart hatches the contact,
a line on the cylinder lying on the face's boundary, a little inside the
face (HatchOnBoundary, 0014 and 0140): one domain from the seam to the
seam, once round. Both its ends lie beside the seam's vertex and are
snapped onto it (SnapToVertex). The vertex's parameters on the wall are
those of one of the seam's two sides; projected onto the line they give
the parameter of whichever end lies on that side. 0149 takes the vertex a
period on where its projection lands on the other end of the line, but
compares with that end's parameter, which is the hatched one only at the
end snapped second. At the end snapped first it is still the data's own
(none yet), so where the vertex lay on the second end's side, the first
end got the second end's parameter, the second end kept its own, and the
piece of no length was dropped: SplitKPart returned success with no piece,
and the walk that followed failed (StartsolFailure). A boss's wall consumed
the same way (0149's case) built where the vertex's parameters lay on the
first end's side.

- SnapToVertex: on a line hatched on the boundary (HatchOnBoundary and
  HatchOffConcaveEdges, whose ends FillSD snaps with their wider
  tolerance), the vertex's copies a period apart on a periodic face are
  tried at either end, not only where the projection lands on the other
  end's parameter. As in 0149, a copy is taken only where it projects
  nearer this end's hatched parameter at no greater distance from the
  line. A snap moves an end by its tolerance at most: where the vertex's
  projection is already the nearest to the end, as on every line that is
  not closed round a periodic face, nothing changes. Ends snapped within a
  vertex's own tolerance (lines hatched in the face) are as before.

The blind hole's rim now builds at its size: the wall gone, the bevel or
rounding meeting the hole's floor at its edge, the volume the exact
shape's.

Upstream: new.
